+This will end the request with `400 - Bad Request` status code and the following [problem details (RFC 9457)](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457) response (`content-type: application/problem+json`):
 
 ```json
 {
-  "date": "2025-06-05T04:20:00.0Z",
+  "type": "about:blank",
+  "title": "Bad Request",
   "status": 400,
+  "detail": "Invalid user input",
   "statusText": "Bad Request",
   "message": "Invalid user input",
   "data": {
     "field": "email"
-  }
+  },
+  "date": "2025-06-05T04:20:00.0Z"
 }
 ```
 
+The format is always enabled (there is no configuration option). `statusText`, `message` and `data` are kept for backward compatibility.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Error responses now use `application/problem+json` instead of `application/json`. Clients that match the exact `content-type` need to be updated. A `content-type` set in the error `headers` still takes precedence.
+
 ### `HTTPError` Fields
 
 - `status`: HTTP status code in the range 200–599.
 - `statusText`: HTTP status text to be sent in the response header.
 - `message`: Error message to be included in the JSON body.
 - `data`: Additional data to be attached under the `data` key in the error JSON body.
-- `body`: Additional top-level properties to be attached in the error JSON body.
+- `body`: Additional top-level properties to be attached in the error JSON body (cannot override `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` and `instance`).
+- `type`: URI reference identifying the problem type (RFC 9457). Defaults to `about:blank` in the JSON body.
+- `instance`: URI reference identifying this occurrence of the problem. Omitted when not set.
 - `headers`: Additional HTTP headers to be sent in the error response.
 - `cause`: The original error object that caused this error, useful for tracing and debugging.
 - `unhandled`: Indicates whether the error was thrown for unknown reasons. See [Unhandled Errors](#unhandled-errors).
